my name is andrew quintman i'm assistant  professor of religious studies in the  department of religious studies at yale  university  i work primarily  in  tibetan cultural areas i work on tibetan  buddhism in tibet northern nepal  northern india and bhutan so i've been  working in the himalayas for close to 25  years now i went for the first time in  the late 1980s i was a study abroad  student as an undergraduate  when i went to northwestern india  and nepal  and i've been engaged ever since  one of the things i i'm most fascinated  by about  religious culture in the tibetan tibetan  buddhist world is the great diversity  you find across  geographic regions and throughout time  so we have this kind of uh image of  tibet through history as being sort of  isolated from the world as being a kind  of  uh hidden valley like a kind of  shangri-la set off  kind of timeless set off geographically  from the rest of the world and of course  we know that that has never been the  case that the himalayas although they  are impressive mountains have always  been permeable and there's always been  movement of people and with people of  religious cultures of religious  practices and religious texts and those  movements have happened in many  directions they've happened  from tibet into china back and forth but  they've also moved especially across the  himalayan  boundaries so from tibet down to nepal  and into india and then of course in the  reverse direction as well and what that  means is that we find uh an incredibly  complex conglomeration of religious  cultures of religious activities of  really just beliefs and practices  throughout the  himalayan regions  we find of course  buddhists  who  are practitioners from the kind of i  don't know mainstream tibetan buddhist  tradition  but there are also buddhists  on the nepali side of the border whose  practices are influenced  from slightly different traditions you  find interactions from those buddhists  with the buddhists of the kathmandu  valley of the newars the traditional  inhabitants of the kathmandu valley but  of course  there are also the pre-buddhist  religions known as bun  which stand on their own but also stand  in conversation  with traditional buddhist practices  and then of course we have uh  hindu traditions and  islam and all of these are sort of  working in in some sense in conversation  of course they're also contested on on  occasion  but they really inhabit this  this kind of giant melting pot if you  will that we call the himalayas the way  that that tibetan buddhism is  traditionally understood comes from the  great histories from the great  narratives that tibetan historians that  tibetan authors  have told themselves and have therefore  told us about their tradition and this  is the  view of tibetan buddhism as a great  tradition that has its history  coming largely from india  through the activities of  important figures from the 7th and 8th  century  a great  kind of tantric sorcerer known as  padmasambhava or guru rinpoche in tibet  is known as the second buddha and also  with a series of  great tibetan kings who were great  sponsors of buddhism in tibet  and yet we know that buddhism developed  in very different ways in very different  sort of locations across the tibetan  cultural world and at different moments  in time and those kind of cultural  pockets those pockets of tibetan  buddhist practice were largely informed  by  the kind of communications  that those inhabitants had with people  from other regions with the specific  teachers who were there  my kind of local developments whether  they be kind of literary developments  based upon certain teachers or upon  based upon kind of local cultural  practices one of the projects i'm  currently involved with is together with  a research partner at the university of  virginia curtis shaffer  that we're calling the life of the  buddha in tibet and it's based around  very very interesting and unique  constellation of visual and literary  materials it's based at the monastery  known as jonang punsa ling just west of  shigatse in western tibet  and it's based around  a series of mural paintings depicting  the life of the buddha in exquisite  detail from the 17th century from the  1620s  it's probably the best preserved and one  of the most extensive  murals depicting the life of the buddha  in existence and is remarkably well  preserved  but together with that we have this  amazing collection of literary materials  describing  not only the life of the buddha but also  the kinds of ritual applications in  which the figure of the historical  buddha  was venerated and was sort of  incorporated into the institutional  life of this monastery so what we're  trying to do is look at the relationship  between this really exquisite  representation of the buddha's life  and the buddha's life as depicted in  literature looking at what are the  relationships what are the differences  between literary and visual applications  of the buddha's life but then trying to  look at the role of the historical  buddha  in the creation of a new institutional  identity for this particular monastery  which itself was  was both very active in terms of its  kind of intellectual life it was the  seat  that created sort of new philosophical  ideas in the history of tibetan buddhism  but it was also politically very active  and had the support of the great  political patrons great  patrons of western tibet and were in  some sense at war  with uh the figures of the dalai lamas  and their own patrons in central tibet  and in in lhasa so my new english  translation of the life of milarepa was  recently published with penguin classics  in 2010 and uh this fall i have a new  book coming out with columbia university  press in their south asia across the  discipline series called the yogan and  the madman reading the biographical  corpus of tibet's great saint milarepa  and both of these books  are centered around uh one of the single  most important figures in tibetan  buddhist history and in fact of the  history of buddhism in the wider  himalayan world and this is the 11th  century  yogin and poet named milarepa  he was a figure who was born right on  the himalayan border on the kind of  northern slope of the himalas in a  valley called kirang he was born in the  11th century and spent  most of his time  studying  and meditating and sort of wandering  across this kind of himalayan landscape  both on the tibetan side but also on the  nepali side as well he visited  kathmandu valley and he also meditated  on the nepali side of the himalayas  for for many many years so the figure of  of milarepa and the life story that's  associated with him was really  transformational in the way that  buddhism developed in the himalayan  world  his life story which was written  almost 400 years after he died  is probably the single best known piece  of tibetan literature  in the world  among tibetan buddhists  and it's become one of the  best loved and best known translations  of tibetan buddhist literature in the  west it was one of the first lenses into  tibetan buddhism  that we had in the west through its  translations uh in the early 20th  century so in my new book i try to look  at the life of milorapa as a literary  work  so to try and think about  one of the most important pieces of  tibetan literature  as a true literary work so trying to  understand uh what were its uh it's sort  of  what was its origin point  how did it develop over time and  especially what did its most famous  author in the 15th century the the  acclaimed madman from tsung or western  tibet what kind of  literary tools and tropes and techniques  did he use to create a work of such  lasting genius  to create a work that tibetans  themselves understood was a work of  genius but was understood even by  readers hundreds of years later and from  cultures very distant from tibet could  also understand as a work of literary  genius one of the interesting things  about the most famous version of the  life of milleripa that was created in  the 15th century by this famous madman  of tsung  was that it was the first to be  disseminated uh through the use of print  medium so this was a period in which  block printing was still  fairly uncommon in central to bed  although it had been incorporated  in china for many centuries and had been  used in the kind of margins of the  tibetan cultural world for quite some  time but it was what the the production  of the print version of the life of  milarepa in the 15th century was one of  the landmarks of book creation in the  tibetan buddhist world  now the madman was  was not only a great literature he was  also a genius in marketing and i like to  think of him as one of tibet's first  multi-media  marketing men  in that together with his text he also  created  a series of kind of  multimedia forms of the yogin's life so  he commissioned a series of paintings  depicting uh miller ape's life for the  first time and he also uh commissioned  a collection of uh ritual practices  that could be practiced along with the  the texts and sort of in in connection  with the artwork so that we have this  package of texts of image and ritual  practices that the author was then  disseminating to the great to his great  patrons and to the great  centers of learning and practice  throughout the tibetan buddhist world so  we see this connection between  literature between art and ritual  practice  you
 

